<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Quitangonha</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1896 CE (approx.) · capital Quitangonha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Swahili sultanate north of Mozambique Island that grew rich on the 19th-century slave trade before Portuguese conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Quitangonha lay on the coast and islands north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique-island">Mozambique Island</a>, around Matibane. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> Muslim families established a ruling house there by the 18th century, closely tied by marriage to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-sancul">Sancul</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-angoche">Angoche</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers held authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coastal towns and drew on alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makua">Makua</a> chiefs inland. In the 19th century Quitangonha became a centre of the clandestine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, shipping captives to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a> and French plantation islands, despite Portuguese and British efforts to suppress it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The sultanate resisted Portuguese control in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makua">Makua</a> chiefs, but it was subdued during Portuguese military campaigns of the mid-1890s and absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1700 CE – 1896 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.53%;width:3.90%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1896 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quitangonha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili, Makua</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Quitangonha, Matibane</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1798&amp;focus=sultanate-of-quitangonha" aria-label="Sultanate of Quitangonha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5731 2702 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Quitangonha lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5861 3386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6051 2958h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6051 2958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mozambique. <a href="/atlas?year=1798&amp;focus=sultanate-of-quitangonha">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

